                <pb facs="0394" n="80" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg80"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/631</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gerard</hi>)</head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-28T19:00:00-05:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 28, 1914, 7 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your August 22. As the United States did not ratify the Congo Act of February 26,
                    1885, relative to the neutralization of the colonies lying within the
                    conventional free-trade zone, it cannot be considered a party to this treaty,
                    and therefore is not in a position to take the action in this matter requested
                    by the German Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
